  "_note": "BROADCASTS x TX POWER. ANTENNA FITTED -- the series baseline; run 1b showed antenna-off is a regime switch, not an attenuator (0/900 at -24 dBm), so half this matrix would be dead cells with it removed.\n\nTests the leading explanation for the project's oldest headline result. The old 'rotation is everything' run (80/92/99% at 4/8/16 s dwell) pinned adv at 2000 ms, so its dwell levels were simultaneously 2/4/8 BROADCASTS PER KEY -- the two factors were perfectly confounded. Runs 1 and 1b then held broadcasts at 5 and found dwell flat twice, the second time at 50% delivery with ~50 points of headroom, which a ceiling cannot explain. This matrix does the complement: dwell is PINNED at 4000 ms and broadcasts vary 1/2/4/8/16 via adv = 4000/bc ms (4000/2000/1000/500/250 ms, all inside the BLE 20..10240 ms range). If broadcast count is the real driver, delivery rises across that axis at fixed dwell -- the reverse of runs 1/1b.\n\nCrossed with TX {+9 dBm, -24 dBm}. Both arms are needed: +9 dBm will likely saturate at high broadcast counts (the anchors' 8 broadcasts already give 99.4% fitted), so -24 dBm is where the knee is measurable -- run 1 put it at 89.0% with room in both directions. 10 conditions x 9 reps = 90 cells + 9 anchors = 99 cells, 1980 keys.\n\nCondition order rotates by one each block so no condition holds a fixed slot; the anchor heads every block. Key rate is ~15/min (20 keys per 80 s cell), well under queue_soft_cap 256, so poller patience is not shortened.",
